# Run the generated application, and the same application interpreted.
#
# Two executables built from one driver (Application.cpp; see Runtime.cmake):
# one linked against the compiled bodies and their entry table, one not. The
# first question is whether they agree - the transcripts must be
# byte-identical, and not empty, or they agree vacuously. The second is whether
# the compiled build compiled anything, and it is the one this test exists for:
# an application that installed nothing and interpreted everything passes the
# first question perfectly. Only the dispatch counters can tell them apart, and
# only if they are asserted: ctbrowser/lib/Script/dispatch.cpp counts every
# crossing between C++, the interpreter and a compiled body, so a strict build
# must show the interpreter never ran.
#
# RUN: ctcompile-test-launcher-vm > %t.vm.out 2> %t.vm.err
# RUN: ctcompile-test-launcher-aot > %t.aot.out 2> %t.aot.err
# RUN: diff %t.vm.out %t.aot.out
# RUN: test -s %t.vm.out
# RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=AOT < %t.aot.err
# RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=VM < %t.vm.err
#
# The count comes from the application, because only it knows how many
# functions its program has; `interpreted 0` is what makes this a strict build
# rather than a hybrid that happened to compile most of it, and a single-entry
# application cannot cross AOT -> AOT, so at least two must be installed.
# AOT-DAG: installed {{([2-9]|[1-9][0-9]+)}} of {{[0-9]+}}, interpreted 0{{$}}
#
# All three of these are ways for the interpreter to be reached, and in an
# AOT-only build every one must be zero - a nonzero VM -> AOT means the
# interpreter entered a compiled body, which means the interpreter was running.
# AOT-DAG: transition "C++ -> VM" = 0{{$}}
# AOT-DAG: transition "VM -> AOT" = 0{{$}}
# AOT-DAG: transition "AOT -> VM" = 0{{$}}
#
# The whole program is one crossing from C++: context::execute asks
# enter_compiled for the top level before it pushes a frame, so a program whose
# top level compiled is entered exactly once and never returns to C++. How
# many times one compiled body calls another is pinned rather than bounded: `>
# 0` would pass on an application whose top level compiled and whose every
# call fell back. 80 is the fixture's own call count - 6 frames of 4 ships,
# plus the scaffolding - and if application.js changes, this changes with it:
# run the aot executable and read the line it prints.
# AOT-DAG: transition "C++ -> AOT" = 1{{$}}
# AOT-DAG: transition "AOT -> AOT" = 80{{$}}
#
# The interpreter-only build: the same driver with nothing linked into it. If
# it also reports compiled crossings, the counters are not measuring what this
# file claims, and every assertion above is about something else.
# VM-DAG: transition "C++ -> AOT" = 0{{$}}
# VM-DAG: transition "VM -> AOT" = 0{{$}}
# VM-DAG: transition "AOT -> AOT" = 0{{$}}
# VM-DAG: transition "AOT -> VM" = 0{{$}}
# VM-DAG: transition "C++ -> VM" = {{[1-9][0-9]*}}
